/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Acts chapters 16-20
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("acts", [
{n:16, name:"The Macedonian Call",
 seal:{ref:"Acts 16:9",verse:"And a vision appeared to Paul in the night; There stood a man of Macedonia, and prayed him, saying, Come over into Macedonia, and help us."},
 facts:["Did you know the first convert in Europe was a businesswoman? Lydia, a seller of purple from Thyatira, was baptized with her household, and her home became the meeting place of the first European church (Acts 16:14-15).","Did you know the jailer's question is the plainest in the Bible? 'Sirs, what must I do to be saved?' drew the plainest answer: 'Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved' (Acts 16:30-31).","Did you know Paul and Silas were Roman citizens? When the magistrates learned they had beaten and jailed Romans without trial, they came in person to apologize and beg them to leave the city (Acts 16:37-39)."],
 questions:[
{q:"Who was the disciple Paul found at Lystra?", options:["Timothy, the son of a believing Jewish woman and a Greek father","Silas, a leader of the Jerusalem church","Titus, a Gentile from Antioch","Luke, the beloved physician"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:1",verse:"Then came he to Derbe and Lystra: and, behold, a certain disciple was there, named Timotheus, the son of a certain woman, which was a Jewess, and believed; but his father was a Greek:", insight:"Timothy came from a mixed home, Jewish mother and Greek father, and God used that very background to open doors for the gospel."},
{q:"Why did Paul circumcise Timothy?", options:["Because Timothy's father was a Greek, and Paul did not want to stumble the Jews who knew it","Because Timothy had asked for it as a sign of faith","Because the apostles in Jerusalem ordered it","Because it was required before baptism"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:3",verse:"Him would Paul have to go forth with him; and took and circumcised him because of the Jews which were in those quarters: for they knew all that his father was a Greek.", insight:"Paul yielded a right he did not need so the gospel would not trip over a needless offense; love sometimes lays down its liberties."},
{q:"What did the Holy Ghost forbid Paul and his company to do?", options:["Preach the word in Asia","Enter the city of Troas","Cross into Macedonia","Visit the churches of Galatia"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:6",verse:"Now when they had gone throughout Phrygia and the region of Galatia, and were forbidden of the Holy Ghost to preach the word in Asia,", insight:"The same Spirit who sends also forbids; a closed door is as much God's guidance as an open one."},
{q:"What happened when they tried to go into Bithynia?", options:["The Spirit suffered them not","An angel appeared and blessed them","They were welcomed with joy","A great storm stopped the ship"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:7",verse:"After they were come to Mysia, they assayed to go into Bithynia: but the Spirit suffered them not.", insight:"God's 'no' protected them from a good plan that was not His plan; trust the hindrance."},
{q:"What did the man in Paul's night vision say?", options:["Come over into Macedonia, and help us","Go back to Jerusalem in peace","Preach the word in Asia","Wait here until I come"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:9",verse:"And a vision appeared to Paul in the night; There stood a man of Macedonia, and prayed him, saying, Come over into Macedonia, and help us.", insight:"One Macedonian's cry in the night turned the whole mission westward; God steers by the prayers of the needy."},
{q:"What did Paul conclude from the vision?", options:["The Lord had called them to preach the gospel in Macedonia","They should return to Antioch for counsel","The vision was only a dream","They must wait for a second sign"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:10",verse:"And after he had seen the vision, immediately we endeavoured to go into Macedonia, assuredly gathering that the Lord had called us for to preach the gospel unto them.", insight:"They 'assuredly gathered' the Lord's call; faith does not wait for thunder when the leading is clear."},
{q:"Which city is called the chief of that part of Macedonia?", options:["Philippi","Neapolis","Samothracia","Amphipolis"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:12",verse:"And from thence to Philippi, which is the chief city of that part of Macedonia, and a colony: and we were in that city abiding certain days.", insight:"God sent them to the chief city; the gospel aims at the centers where the word will travel farthest."},
{q:"Where did Paul and his company go on the sabbath day?", options:["Out of the city by a river side, where prayer was wont to be made","To the synagogue in the marketplace","To the house of Lydia","Up to the city walls"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:13",verse:"And on the sabbath we went out of the city by a river side, where prayer was wont to be made; and we sat down, and spake unto the women which resorted thither.", insight:"They looked for prayer where prayer was known to gather; go where seekers already meet God."},
{q:"Who was Lydia?", options:["A seller of purple from Thyatira, who worshipped God","A prophetess of the Macedonian temple","A Roman magistrate's wife","A servant girl with a spirit of divination"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:14",verse:"And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple, of the city of Thyatira, which worshipped God, heard us: whose heart the Lord opened, that she attended unto the things which were spoken of Paul.", insight:"Lydia was a businesswoman and a worshipper; God often reaches the market through a heart already reaching for Him."},
{q:"What opened Lydia's heart to Paul's message?", options:["The Lord opened her heart, that she attended to Paul's words","Her years of study in Thyatira","A dream she had the night before","The persuasive skill of Silas"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:14",verse:"And a certain woman named Lydia, a seller of purple, of the city of Thyatira, which worshipped God, heard us: whose heart the Lord opened, that she attended unto the things which were spoken of Paul.", insight:"Only the Lord can open a heart; our part is to speak, His is to make the hearing live."},
{q:"What did the damsel with the spirit of divination bring her masters?", options:["Much gain by soothsaying","Silver shrines for Diana","News from the magistrates","Purple cloth from Thyatira"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:16",verse:"And it came to pass, as we went to prayer, a certain damsel possessed with a spirit of divination met us, which brought her masters much gain by soothsaying:", insight:"Evil can wear a profitable face; not every gain is a blessing."},
{q:"In whose name did Paul command the spirit to leave the damsel?", options:["In the name of Jesus Christ","In the name of the apostles","By the authority of Rome","In his own name as a prophet"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:18",verse:"And this did she many days. But Paul, being grieved, turned and said to the spirit, I command thee in the name of Jesus Christ to come out of her. And he came out the same hour.", insight:"The name of Jesus Christ, not Paul's, broke the chain; power for ministry is borrowed, never owned."},
{q:"What charge did the masters bring against Paul and Silas?", options:["They teach customs not lawful for Romans to receive or observe","They stole money from the marketplace","They refused to pay the temple tax","They plotted against Caesar"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:21",verse:"And teach customs, which are not lawful for us to receive, neither to observe, being Romans.", insight:"They framed a spiritual mission as a civic crime; the gospel will always be misread by those it threatens."},
{q:"Where did the jailer thrust Paul and Silas?", options:["Into the inner prison, with their feet fast in the stocks","Into the courtyard under guard","Into a room above the gate","Into the magistrates' own house"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:24",verse:"Who, having received such a charge, thrust them into the inner prison, and made their feet fast in the stocks.", insight:"The inner prison could not hold the inner song; God is nearest when the stocks are tightest."},
{q:"What were Paul and Silas doing at midnight in prison?", options:["They prayed, and sang praises unto God","They slept through their suffering","They plotted their escape","They argued with the jailer"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:25",verse:"And at midnight Paul and Silas prayed, and sang praises unto God: and the prisoners heard them.", insight:"Midnight praise turns a prison into a chapel; worship is loudest where hope seems gone."},
{q:"What did the earthquake do?", options:["It shook the prison's foundations, opened all the doors, and loosed every band","It threw down the city walls","It killed the jailer in his sleep","It only rattled the windows"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:26",verse:"And suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken: and immediately all the doors were opened, and every one’s bands were loosed.", insight:"God shook the building to open a heart; sometimes He moves the earth to move one man."},
{q:"What did Paul cry to the jailer who would have killed himself?", options:["Do thyself no harm: for we are all here","Flee with us before the magistrates come","Your sword cannot hurt a servant of God","Call the magistrates at once"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:28",verse:"But Paul cried with a loud voice, saying, Do thyself no harm: for we are all here.", insight:"Paul saved the life of the man who chained him; mercy answers cruelty by protecting the cruel."},
{q:"What did the trembling jailer ask Paul and Silas?", options:["Sirs, what must I do to be saved?","How did you escape your chains?","Will the magistrates punish me?","Where should I go tonight?"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:30",verse:"And brought them out, and said, Sirs, what must I do to be saved?", insight:"Terror became the door to truth; the jailer asked the one question every heart must ask."},
{q:"What was their answer to the jailer?", options:["Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved, and thy house","Be baptized first, and then believe","Keep the law of Moses perfectly","Offer a sacrifice at the temple"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:31",verse:"And they said, Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved, and thy house.", insight:"The whole gospel in one sentence: believe on the Lord Jesus Christ; salvation is a Person received, not a price paid."},
{q:"Why did the magistrates fear when they heard Paul and Silas were Romans?", options:["They had beaten them openly and uncondemned, against Roman law","They had stolen Roman property","They had insulted Caesar's image","They had bribed the serjeants"], a:0, ref:"Acts 16:38",verse:"And the serjeants told these words unto the magistrates: and they feared, when they heard that they were Romans.", insight:"Unjust power fears being exposed; truth about who you are can unsettle a whole court."}
]},
{n:17, name:"The Unknown God",
 seal:{ref:"Acts 17:11",verse:"These were more noble than those in Thessalonica, in that they received the word with all readiness of mind, and searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so."},
 facts:["Did you know the Athenians kept an altar 'To the Unknown God' in case they had missed one? Paul took their own caution and used it to preach the God they worshipped without knowing (Acts 17:23).","Did you know the mob's charge is one of the earliest outsider descriptions of the church? 'These that have turned the world upside down are come hither also' (Acts 17:6).","Did you know Paul quoted the Athenians' own poets on Mars' hill? 'For we are also his offspring' came from their literature, and he used it to argue for the living God (Acts 17:28)."],
 questions:[
{q:"For how many sabbath days did Paul reason in the Thessalonian synagogue?", options:["Three","Seven","Ten","Forty"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:2",verse:"And Paul, as his manner was, went in unto them, and three sabbath days reasoned with them out of the scriptures,", insight:"Paul reasoned from the Scriptures, not from slogans; persuasion in God's work is patient and biblical."},
{q:"What did Paul open and allege from the Scriptures?", options:["That Christ must needs have suffered, and risen again from the dead","That the law of Moses was ended forever","That the Romans would soon fall","That the temple must be rebuilt"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:3",verse:"Opening and alleging, that Christ must needs have suffered, and risen again from the dead; and that this Jesus, whom I preach unto you, is Christ.", insight:"The cross and the empty tomb are the heart of it all; every gospel sermon lands there."},
{q:"Who consorted with Paul and Silas at Thessalonica?", options:["Some of the Jews, a great multitude of devout Greeks, and chief women not a few","Only the poor of the city","The magistrates and soldiers","The philosophers of the Areopagus"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:4",verse:"And some of them believed, and consorted with Paul and Silas; and of the devout Greeks a great multitude, and of the chief women not a few.", insight:"The gospel drew Jews, Greeks, and leading women together; Christ makes one family of unlikely people."},
{q:"What did the unbelieving Jews do?", options:["They gathered lewd fellows, set the city in an uproar, and assaulted the house of Jason","They debated Paul peacefully in the synagogue","They wrote a letter to Caesar","They left the city in silence"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:5",verse:"But the Jews which believed not, moved with envy, took unto them certain lewd fellows of the baser sort, and gathered a company, and set all the city on an uproar, and assaulted the house of Jason, and sought to bring them out to the people.", insight:"Envy hires a mob when it cannot answer an argument; noise is often the confession of a lost case."},
{q:"What did the mob cry about the apostles?", options:["These that have turned the world upside down are come hither also","These men have stolen from the temple","These Jews must leave our city","These strangers bring a plague"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:6",verse:"And when they found them not, they drew Jason and certain brethren unto the rulers of the city, crying, These that have turned the world upside down are come hither also;", insight:"The world called the gospel world turning; when Christ enters a life, things rightly turn over."},
{q:"What was the charge against the Christians?", options:["They do contrary to Caesar's decrees, saying there is another king, one Jesus","They refuse to pay taxes","They practice witchcraft","They burn the synagogues"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:7",verse:"Whom Jason hath received: and these all do contrary to the decrees of Caesar, saying that there is another king, one Jesus.", insight:"They named Jesus as King; every true confession of Christ carries a quiet challenge to every rival throne."},
{q:"What did the rulers do to Jason?", options:["They took security of him, and let him go","They cast him into prison for life","They beat him with many stripes","They sent him to Rome"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:9",verse:"And when they had taken security of Jason, and of the other, they let them go.", insight:"Jason paid a price for hosting the gospel; hospitality for Christ sometimes costs, and it is never wasted."},
{q:"Where did the brethren send Paul and Silas by night?", options:["To Berea","To Athens","Back to Philippi","To Corinth"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:10",verse:"And the brethren immediately sent away Paul and Silas by night unto Berea: who coming thither went into the synagogue of the Jews.", insight:"Wisdom sometimes withdraws by night; leaving a riot is not cowardice but care for the flock."},
{q:"Why were the Bereans more noble than those in Thessalonica?", options:["They received the word with readiness of mind, and searched the scriptures daily","They were richer and better educated","They had no synagogue of their own","They believed without any questions"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:11",verse:"These were more noble than those in Thessalonica, in that they received the word with all readiness of mind, and searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so.", insight:"Nobility in God's sight is a Bible searched daily; test even an apostle's preaching by the Scriptures."},
{q:"What came of the Bereans' searching?", options:["Many of them believed, with honourable Greek women and men not a few","They rejected Paul as a deceiver","They asked Paul to leave the city","They reported him to the magistrates"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:12",verse:"Therefore many of them believed; also of honourable women which were Greeks, and of men, not a few.", insight:"Searching the Scriptures ends in believing; an honest open Bible rarely leaves a heart unchanged."},
{q:"Where did the brethren send Paul next, while Silas and Timothy stayed behind?", options:["As it were to the sea, on the way to Athens","Into the wilderness of Judea","Back to Antioch by ship","To the island of Crete"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:14",verse:"And then immediately the brethren sent away Paul to go as it were to the sea: but Silas and Timotheus abode there still.", insight:"God separated the team for a season; His work does not depend on keeping everyone together."},
{q:"What stirred Paul's spirit in Athens?", options:["He saw the city wholly given to idolatry","He was mocked by the philosophers","He found no synagogue there","He was beaten by the magistrates"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:16",verse:"Now while Paul waited for them at Athens, his spirit was stirred in him, when he saw the city wholly given to idolatry.", insight:"Idols stirred not his contempt but his spirit; a city lost to false gods should move us, not amuse us."},
{q:"Which philosophers encountered Paul at Athens?", options:["The Epicureans and the Stoics","The Platonists and the Sophists","The Cynics and the Skeptics","The Peripatetics and the Pythagoreans"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:18",verse:"Then certain philosophers of the Epicureans, and of the Stoicks, encountered him. And some said, What will this babbler say? other some, He seemeth to be a setter forth of strange gods: because he preached unto them Jesus, and the resurrection.", insight:"The gospel met the best minds of Athens head on; truth does not fear the philosophers."},
{q:"What did some Athenians call Paul?", options:["A babbler","A sorcerer","A tax collector","A Roman spy"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:18",verse:"Then certain philosophers of the Epicureans, and of the Stoicks, encountered him. And some said, What will this babbler say? other some, He seemeth to be a setter forth of strange gods: because he preached unto them Jesus, and the resurrection.", insight:"The world calls God's messenger a babbler; do not measure your calling by the crowd's nickname."},
{q:"Where did they bring Paul to hear his doctrine?", options:["Unto Areopagus, Mars' hill","To the temple of Diana","To the house of Jason","To the prison of the city"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:19",verse:"And they took him, and brought him unto Areopagus, saying, May we know what this new doctrine, whereof thou speakest, is?", insight:"They took him to the seat of their wisdom; God turns the world's courtroom into His pulpit."},
{q:"What was written on the altar Paul found in Athens?", options:["TO THE UNKNOWN GOD","TO ZEUS THE KING","TO THE GOD OF THE JEWS","TO FORTUNE AND FATE"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:23",verse:"For as I passed by, and beheld your devotions, I found an altar with this inscription, TO THE UNKNOWN GOD. Whom therefore ye ignorantly worship, him declare I unto you.", insight:"Their own altar confessed their ignorance; God had left them a blank space that only He could fill."},
{q:"Where does God not dwell, Paul told them?", options:["In temples made with hands","In the hearts of men","In the heavens above","In the city of Athens"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:24",verse:"God that made the world and all things therein, seeing that he is Lord of heaven and earth, dwelleth not in temples made with hands;", insight:"The Maker of heaven and earth cannot be housed by hands; worship Him too big for buildings."},
{q:"Whose words did Paul quote to the Athenians?", options:["Their own poets: 'For we are also his offspring'","The prophet Isaiah","The psalms of David","The sayings of Socrates"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:28",verse:"For in him we live, and move, and have our being; as certain also of your own poets have said, For we are also his offspring.", insight:"Paul met them on their own ground, quoting their poets; wisdom learns the language of its hearers."},
{q:"What does God now command all men everywhere?", options:["To repent","To offer sacrifices","To build temples","To keep the sabbaths"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:30",verse:"And the times of this ignorance God winked at; but now commandeth all men every where to repent:", insight:"God winked at past ignorance, but now commands repentance everywhere; mercy puts an end to delay."},
{q:"Who are named as believing in Athens?", options:["Dionysius the Areopagite, and a woman named Damaris","Jason and his household","The Epicurean philosophers","The rulers of the city"], a:0, ref:"Acts 17:34",verse:"Howbeit certain men clave unto him, and believed: among the which was Dionysius the Areopagite, and a woman named Damaris, and others with them.", insight:"Even in proud Athens, some clave to Paul and believed; no city is too hard for the gospel."}
]},
{n:18, name:"Paul in Corinth",
 seal:{ref:"Acts 18:10",verse:"For I am with thee, and no man shall set on thee to hurt thee: for I have much people in this city."},
 facts:["Did you know Paul worked as a tentmaker while he preached? He lived and labored with Aquila and Priscilla, 'for by their occupation they were tentmakers,' and reasoned in the synagogue every sabbath (Acts 18:3-4).","Did you know the judge who dismissed the case against Paul was Gallio, the brother of the famous philosopher Seneca? Roman records mention him, which helps date Paul's eighteen months in Corinth (Acts 18:11-12).","Did you know Apollos was eloquent and 'mighty in the scriptures' yet only knew the baptism of John? Even a brilliant teacher needed the fuller story, and two tentmakers gave it to him (Acts 18:24-26)."],
 questions:[
{q:"Who was Aquila?", options:["A Jew born in Pontus, lately come from Italy with his wife Priscilla","A Roman centurion stationed at Corinth","A Greek philosopher of Athens","A silversmith of Ephesus"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:2",verse:"And found a certain Jew named Aquila, born in Pontus, lately come from Italy, with his wife Priscilla; (because that Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome:) and came unto them.", insight:"God gave Paul a home and a trade partner before He gave him a congregation; provision precedes the work."},
{q:"Why had Aquila and Priscilla come from Italy?", options:["Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome","They were fleeing a famine","Paul had sent for them","They sought work in Corinth"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:2",verse:"And found a certain Jew named Aquila, born in Pontus, lately come from Italy, with his wife Priscilla; (because that Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome:) and came unto them.", insight:"An emperor's decree became God's relocation plan; even hostile politics serve the mission."},
{q:"What was the occupation of Paul, Aquila, and Priscilla?", options:["Tentmakers","Fishermen","Scribes","Silversmiths"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:3",verse:"And because he was of the same craft, he abode with them, and wrought: for by their occupation they were tentmakers.", insight:"The apostle worked with his hands; ministry that labors is ministry that lasts."},
{q:"What did Paul do when Silas and Timothy arrived from Macedonia?", options:["He was pressed in the spirit, and testified to the Jews that Jesus was Christ","He left Corinth at once","He stopped preaching to rest","He wrote a letter to Rome"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:5",verse:"And when Silas and Timotheus were come from Macedonia, Paul was pressed in the spirit, and testified to the Jews that Jesus was Christ.", insight:"The arrival of friends rekindled the fire; God often renews us through the brethren."},
{q:"What did Paul say when the Jews opposed and blasphemed?", options:["From henceforth I will go unto the Gentiles","I will never preach again","I will return to Jerusalem","I will appeal to Caesar"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:6",verse:"And when they opposed themselves, and blasphemed, he shook his raiment, and said unto them, Your blood be upon your own heads; I am clean: from henceforth I will go unto the Gentiles.", insight:"Rejection by some opened the door to others; when one field hardens, sow the next."},
{q:"Into whose house did Paul go after leaving the synagogue?", options:["Justus, one that worshipped God, whose house joined hard to the synagogue","Jason of Thessalonica","Lydia of Philippi","Gaius of Derbe"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:7",verse:"And he departed thence, and entered into a certain man’s house, named Justus, one that worshipped God, whose house joined hard to the synagogue.", insight:"God planted the church next door to the synagogue; the gospel stays close to those who refuse it."},
{q:"Who was Crispus?", options:["The chief ruler of the synagogue, who believed on the Lord with all his house","A Roman deputy of Achaia","A tentmaker from Pontus","An eloquent Jew from Alexandria"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:8",verse:"And Crispus, the chief ruler of the synagogue, believed on the Lord with all his house; and many of the Corinthians hearing believed, and were baptized.", insight:"The synagogue's own ruler believed; God sometimes saves the very leader of the opposition."},
{q:"What did the Lord say to Paul in the night vision?", options:["Be not afraid, but speak, and hold not thy peace","Flee from Corinth by morning","Your work here is finished","Send Silas to Macedonia"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:9",verse:"Then spake the Lord to Paul in the night by a vision, Be not afraid, but speak, and hold not thy peace:", insight:"Fear told him to be silent; the Lord told him to speak. Obey the voice from heaven, not the fear in your chest."},
{q:"What promise did the Lord give Paul in Corinth?", options:["I am with thee, and I have much people in this city","No one in Corinth will believe","You will soon leave in chains","An earthquake will free you"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:10",verse:"For I am with thee, and no man shall set on thee to hurt thee: for I have much people in this city.", insight:"Christ had 'much people' in a pagan city before Paul met one; the harvest is appointed before the preaching."},
{q:"How long did Paul continue in Corinth?", options:["A year and six months","Three months","Two years","Forty days"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:11",verse:"And he continued there a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them.", insight:"Eighteen months of steady teaching; depth comes from staying, not from rushing."},
{q:"Who was the deputy of Achaia?", options:["Gallio","Felix","Festus","Sergius Paulus"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:12",verse:"And when Gallio was the deputy of Achaia, the Jews made insurrection with one accord against Paul, and brought him to the judgment seat,", insight:"God set a fair judge on the bench at the right moment; providence staffs the courtroom too."},
{q:"What did the Jews accuse Paul of before Gallio?", options:["Persuading men to worship God contrary to the law","Stealing from the temple treasury","Plotting rebellion against Rome","Practicing magic in the city"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:13",verse:"Saying, This fellow persuadeth men to worship God contrary to the law.", insight:"They dressed doctrine as lawbreaking; when men cannot refute the message, they attack the messenger."},
{q:"How did Gallio answer the Jews?", options:["If it be a question of words and names, and of your law, look ye to it","I will hear this case at Rome","Paul is guilty and must be beaten","I will consult the high priest"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:15",verse:"But if it be a question of words and names, and of your law, look ye to it; for I will be no judge of such matters.", insight:"Gallio refused to judge what he did not understand; sometimes God protects His servants through a judge's indifference."},
{q:"What happened to Sosthenes before the judgment seat?", options:["The Greeks beat him, and Gallio cared for none of those things","He was crowned by the magistrates","He fled to Ephesus","He believed on the Lord"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:17",verse:"Then all the Greeks took Sosthenes, the chief ruler of the synagogue, and beat him before the judgment seat. And Gallio cared for none of those things.", insight:"Gallio's indifference did not mean God's; heaven keeps a record when earth looks away."},
{q:"Where did Paul shave his head, and why?", options:["At Cenchrea, for he had a vow","At Corinth, in mourning","At Ephesus, in sickness","At Troas, before sailing"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:18",verse:"And Paul after this tarried there yet a good while, and then took his leave of the brethren, and sailed thence into Syria, and with him Priscilla and Aquila; having shorn his head in Cenchrea: for he had a vow.", insight:"Even the apostle to the Gentiles kept a Jewish vow; honoring your roots need not hinder your mission."},
{q:"What did Paul tell the Ephesians when they asked him to stay longer?", options:["I must by all means keep this feast at Jerusalem, but I will return if God will","I will never come back to Asia","I am too old to travel further","My work in Ephesus is done"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:21",verse:"But bade them farewell, saying, I must by all means keep this feast that cometh in Jerusalem: but I will return again unto you, if God will. And he sailed from Ephesus.", insight:"Every plan ended with 'if God will'; make your appointments subject to heaven."},
{q:"Where did Paul go after leaving Ephesus?", options:["He landed at Caesarea, saluted the church, and went down to Antioch","He sailed straight to Rome","He returned to Philippi","He crossed into Bithynia"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:22",verse:"And when he had landed at Caesarea, and gone up, and saluted the church, he went down to Antioch.", insight:"He saluted the church on his way; a traveling servant keeps his ties to home."},
{q:"What did Paul do throughout Galatia and Phrygia?", options:["He went over the country in order, strengthening all the disciples","He planted no churches there","He collected money for Jerusalem","He argued with the Judaizers"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:23",verse:"And after he had spent some time there, he departed, and went over all the country of Galatia and Phrygia in order, strengthening all the disciples.", insight:"Strengthening disciples is as apostolic as winning converts; the church needs building up, not only adding to."},
{q:"Who was Apollos?", options:["A Jew born at Alexandria, eloquent and mighty in the scriptures","A Roman soldier converted at Philippi","A servant of the high priest","A silversmith of Ephesus"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:24",verse:"And a certain Jew named Apollos, born at Alexandria, an eloquent man, and mighty in the scriptures, came to Ephesus.", insight:"Eloquence plus Scripture is a gift; Apollos shows that talent serves truth best."},
{q:"Who expounded the way of God more perfectly to Apollos?", options:["Aquila and Priscilla","Paul and Silas","Peter and John","Barnabas and Mark"], a:0, ref:"Acts 18:26",verse:"And he began to speak boldly in the synagogue: whom when Aquila and Priscilla had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly.", insight:"Two tentmakers tutored the eloquent preacher; God often teaches the gifted through the humble."}
]},
{n:19, name:"The Riot at Ephesus",
 seal:{ref:"Acts 19:20",verse:"So mightily grew the word of God and prevailed."},
 facts:["Did you know the burned magic books at Ephesus were valued at fifty thousand pieces of silver? That was a fortune in ancient wages, showing how completely the converts broke with their old craft (Acts 19:19).","Did you know the evil spirit told the sons of Sceva, 'Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are ye?' Even demons could tell borrowed words from real authority (Acts 19:15).","Did you know the great riot at Ephesus began with a silversmith's bottom line? Demetrius admitted, 'by this craft we have our wealth,' and the city spent two hours shouting for Diana (Acts 19:25, 34)."],
 questions:[
{q:"What did the Ephesian disciples say about the Holy Ghost?", options:["We have not so much as heard whether there be any Holy Ghost","We received Him at Pentecost","We were filled at our baptism","We do not believe in Him"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:2",verse:"He said unto them, Have ye received the Holy Ghost since ye believed? And they said unto him, We have not so much as heard whether there be any Holy Ghost.", insight:"They believed without knowing the Spirit; honest ignorance is the doorway to fuller grace."},
{q:"In whose name were they then baptized?", options:["In the name of the Lord Jesus","In the name of John only","In the name of Paul","In the name of the synagogue"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:5",verse:"When they heard this, they were baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus.", insight:"John's baptism pointed forward; Christian baptism plants you in Christ Himself."},
{q:"What happened when Paul laid his hands on them?", options:["The Holy Ghost came on them, and they spake with tongues and prophesied","Nothing happened at all","They fell into a deep sleep","They were struck blind"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:6",verse:"And when Paul had laid his hands upon them, the Holy Ghost came on them; and they spake with tongues, and prophesied.", insight:"The laying on of hands carried the Spirit's filling; God still meets obedient faith with His presence."},
{q:"How many men were in this company?", options:["About twelve","About seventy","About three thousand","About forty"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:7",verse:"And all the men were about twelve.", insight:"Twelve men, like twelve apostles; God loves to start movements with a small, full number."},
{q:"How long did Paul speak boldly in the Ephesian synagogue?", options:["For the space of three months","For one sabbath only","For forty days","For a whole year"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:8",verse:"And he went into the synagogue, and spake boldly for the space of three months, disputing and persuading the things concerning the kingdom of God.", insight:"Three months of bold, persuading speech; Paul gave the synagogue every chance before he moved on."},
{q:"Where did Paul dispute daily after leaving the synagogue?", options:["In the school of Tyrannus","In the temple of Diana","In the house of Aquila","In the marketplace"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:9",verse:"But when divers were hardened, and believed not, but spake evil of that way before the multitude, he departed from them, and separated the disciples, disputing daily in the school of one Tyrannus.", insight:"A lecture hall became a church; God will use any rented room that is yielded to Him."},
{q:"How long did the work at Ephesus continue?", options:["Two years, so that all in Asia heard the word of the Lord","Three months only","Seven years","One sabbath"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:10",verse:"And this continued by the space of two years; so that all they which dwelt in Asia heard the word of the Lord Jesus, both Jews and Greeks.", insight:"Two years of daily teaching reached a whole province; steady sowing fills a region."},
{q:"What did God work by the hands of Paul?", options:["Special miracles","Ordinary healings only","Visions for the elders","Dreams for the disciples"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:11",verse:"And God wrought special miracles by the hands of Paul:", insight:"'Special miracles' for a city steeped in magic; God outshines the darkness on its own ground."},
{q:"What was carried from Paul's body to the sick?", options:["Handkerchiefs or aprons","His sandals and cloak","Letters from Jerusalem","Water from the river"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:12",verse:"So that from his body were brought unto the sick handkerchiefs or aprons, and the diseases departed from them, and the evil spirits went out of them.", insight:"Even cloth that touched Paul carried healing; God's power overflows the vessel that bears it."},
{q:"Who tried to cast out evil spirits by the name of Jesus?", options:["Seven sons of Sceva, a chief of the priests","The sons of Zebedee","Aquila and Priscilla","The town clerk's officers"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:14",verse:"And there were seven sons of one Sceva, a Jew, and chief of the priests, which did so.", insight:"They borrowed a name they did not know; religion without relationship is an empty formula."},
{q:"What did the evil spirit answer them?", options:["Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are ye?","I will obey the sons of Sceva","Depart from this house in peace","Your faith has made you whole"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:15",verse:"And the evil spirit answered and said, Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are ye?", insight:"Even demons could tell the difference between knowing about Jesus and knowing Jesus."},
{q:"What happened to the seven sons?", options:["The possessed man overcame them, and they fled naked and wounded","They were arrested by the magistrates","They became disciples of Paul","They were praised by the people"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:16",verse:"And the man in whom the evil spirit was leaped on them, and overcame them, and prevailed against them, so that they fled out of that house naked and wounded.", insight:"They went to fight a battle they had no armor for; never wield a weapon you have not been given."},
{q:"What was the result throughout Ephesus?", options:["Fear fell on all, and the name of the Lord Jesus was magnified","The city mocked the disciples","Paul was driven out","The synagogue closed its doors"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:17",verse:"And this was known to all the Jews and Greeks also dwelling at Ephesus; and fear fell on them all, and the name of the Lord Jesus was magnified.", insight:"God turned a humiliation into His advertisement; the name of Jesus grew greater through their failure."},
{q:"What did the converted sorcerers do with their books?", options:["They brought them together and burned them before all men","They sold them to buy food for the poor","They hid them in the temple","They gave them to Paul"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:19",verse:"Many of them also which used curious arts brought their books together, and burned them before all men: and they counted the price of them, and found it fifty thousand pieces of silver.", insight:"True repentance burns the bridges; they did not sell their sin, they destroyed it in the open."},
{q:"What was the price of the burned books?", options:["Fifty thousand pieces of silver","Five thousand pieces of silver","One hundred talents of gold","Thirty pieces of silver"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:19",verse:"Many of them also which used curious arts brought their books together, and burned them before all men: and they counted the price of them, and found it fifty thousand pieces of silver.", insight:"Fifty thousand pieces of silver went up in smoke; following Christ cost them a fortune, and they counted it gain."},
{q:"Who was Demetrius?", options:["A silversmith who made silver shrines for Diana","A chief priest of the Jews","A Roman deputy of Asia","A disciple of John"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:24",verse:"For a certain man named Demetrius, a silversmith, which made silver shrines for Diana, brought no small gain unto the craftsmen;", insight:"His religion was his revenue; when the gospel touched his trade, his true god showed."},
{q:"What was Demetrius's complaint against Paul?", options:["Paul persuaded many, saying they be no gods which are made with hands","Paul stole silver from the craftsmen","Paul refused to buy the shrines","Paul preached against the emperor"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:26",verse:"Moreover ye see and hear, that not alone at Ephesus, but almost throughout all Asia, this Paul hath persuaded and turned away much people, saying that they be no gods, which are made with hands:", insight:"Paul's preaching emptied the idol market; the gospel is bad news for every business built on lies."},
{q:"What did the angry crowd cry out?", options:["Great is Diana of the Ephesians","Great is Paul of Tarsus","Death to the Christians","Long live the emperor"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:28",verse:"And when they heard these sayings, they were full of wrath, and cried out, saying, Great is Diana of the Ephesians.", insight:"They shouted a slogan instead of thinking; mobs chant what they cannot defend."},
{q:"How long did the crowd cry out in the theatre?", options:["About the space of two hours","Until midnight","For three days","About half an hour"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:34",verse:"But when they knew that he was a Jew, all with one voice about the space of two hours cried out, Great is Diana of the Ephesians.", insight:"Two hours of shouting proved nothing but their confusion; noise is not evidence."},
{q:"What did the town clerk tell the crowd?", options:["To be quiet and do nothing rashly","To seize Paul and Silas","To burn the theatre down","To march on the synagogue"], a:0, ref:"Acts 19:36",verse:"Seeing then that these things cannot be spoken against, ye ought to be quiet, and to do nothing rashly.", insight:"A calm clerk did what a raging mob could not; God sometimes quiets a riot with a reasonable voice."}
]},
{n:20, name:"Farewell to the Ephesians",
 seal:{ref:"Acts 20:24",verse:"But none of these things move me, neither count I my life dear unto myself, so that I might finish my course with joy, and the ministry, which I have received of the Lord Jesus, to testify the gospel of the grace of God."},
 facts:["Did you know Paul's farewell at Miletus preserves a saying of Jesus found nowhere in the Gospels? 'It is more blessed to give than to receive' appears only here in all of Scripture (Acts 20:35).","Did you know Eutychus fell from a third story window while Paul preached until midnight? Paul embraced him as the prophets of old had done, and the young man was brought up alive (Acts 20:9-10).","Did you know the very name of this game comes from this chapter? Paul told the Ephesian elders, 'I have not shunned to declare unto you all the counsel of God' (Acts 20:27)."],
 questions:[
{q:"Why did Paul change his travel plans in Greece?", options:["The Jews laid wait for him as he was about to sail into Syria","A storm destroyed his ship","He received a vision of Macedonia","The brethren in Corinth sent him away"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:3",verse:"And there abode three months. And when the Jews laid wait for him, as he was about to sail into Syria, he purposed to return through Macedonia.", insight:"A plot changed his route but not his mission; God's servant adapts the path, never the purpose."},
{q:"Which man of Berea accompanied Paul into Asia?", options:["Sopater","Secundus","Tychicus","Trophimus"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:4",verse:"And there accompanied him into Asia Sopater of Berea; and of the Thessalonians, Aristarchus and Secundus; and Gaius of Derbe, and Timotheus; and of Asia, Tychicus and Trophimus.", insight:"God surrounds His servants with faithful companions; no one finishes the course alone."},
{q:"How long did the voyage from Philippi to Troas take?", options:["Five days","Three days","Seven days","Forty days"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:6",verse:"And we sailed away from Philippi after the days of unleavened bread, and came unto them to Troas in five days; where we abode seven days.", insight:"Five days at sea, seven days in Troas; the mission moves at God's pace, not ours."},
{q:"Until what hour did Paul preach at Troas?", options:["Until midnight","Until sunrise","Until the third hour","Until evening"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:7",verse:"And upon the first day of the week, when the disciples came together to break bread, Paul preached unto them, ready to depart on the morrow; and continued his speech until midnight.", insight:"He preached till midnight, ready to depart; urgency for souls can stretch the clock."},
{q:"Who was Eutychus?", options:["A young man who sat in a window and fell asleep during Paul's long preaching","A deacon of the church at Troas","A Roman soldier guarding the door","A merchant from Philippi"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:9",verse:"And there sat in a window a certain young man named Eutychus, being fallen into a deep sleep: and as Paul was long preaching, he sunk down with sleep, and fell down from the third loft, and was taken up dead.", insight:"A sleepy listener in a window seat; even under the best preaching, we must watch our own hearts."},
{q:"What did Paul say when they found Eutychus dead?", options:["Trouble not yourselves; for his life is in him","Carry him out and bury him","This is God's judgment on sleepers","Let us pray until morning"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:10",verse:"And Paul went down, and fell on him, and embracing him said, Trouble not yourselves; for his life is in him.", insight:"Paul embraced the dead boy in faith; love acts before it explains."},
{q:"How were the disciples comforted?", options:["They brought the young man alive, and were not a little comforted","They received a letter from Jerusalem","Paul promised to stay longer","An angel appeared to them"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:12",verse:"And they brought the young man alive, and were not a little comforted.", insight:"'Not a little comforted' is Luke's understatement for overflowing joy; God restores what seemed lost."},
{q:"Why did Paul go afoot to Assos?", options:["For so had he appointed, minding himself to go afoot","He had no money for the ship","The ship was too full","He was fleeing the Jews"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:13",verse:"And we went before to ship, and sailed unto Assos, there intending to take in Paul: for so had he appointed, minding himself to go afoot.", insight:"The apostle chose the lonely road on foot; sometimes God walks with us apart from the crowd."},
{q:"Why did Paul sail past Ephesus?", options:["He would not spend the time in Asia, for he hasted to be at Jerusalem for Pentecost","He was angry with the Ephesians","The ship could not land there","He had forgotten his promise"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:16",verse:"For Paul had determined to sail by Ephesus, because he would not spend the time in Asia: for he hasted, if it were possible for him, to be at Jerusalem the day of Pentecost.", insight:"He skipped a beloved church to keep an appointment with God; love for people never outranks obedience."},
{q:"Whom did Paul call to him from Miletus?", options:["The elders of the church at Ephesus","The apostles at Jerusalem","The deacons of Troas","The rulers of the synagogue"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:17",verse:"And from Miletus he sent to Ephesus, and called the elders of the church.", insight:"He summoned the shepherds, not the crowd; leadership carries the weight of farewell."},
{q:"How did Paul describe his service among them?", options:["With all humility of mind, and with many tears","With power and great wealth","With sternness and authority","With ease and comfort"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:19",verse:"Serving the Lord with all humility of mind, and with many tears, and temptations, which befell me by the lying in wait of the Jews:", insight:"Humility and tears marked his ministry; true strength in God's work weeps."},
{q:"How had Paul taught them?", options:["Publicly and from house to house, keeping back nothing profitable","Only in the synagogue on sabbaths","Through letters alone","In parables they could not understand"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:20",verse:"And how I kept back nothing that was profitable unto you, but have shewed you, and have taught you publickly, and from house to house,", insight:"Nothing profitable was kept back; a faithful teacher hides no truth to keep friends."},
{q:"How did Paul describe his journey to Jerusalem?", options:["I go bound in the spirit, not knowing what shall befall me there","I go with joy, knowing all will be well","I go reluctantly, fearing the Jews","I go because the brethren commanded me"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:22",verse:"And now, behold, I go bound in the spirit unto Jerusalem, not knowing the things that shall befall me there:", insight:"Bound in spirit, not knowing the future; obedience does not require a map, only a Master."},
{q:"What did Paul say about his own life?", options:["He counted it not dear to himself, so he might finish his course with joy","He would protect it at all costs","He had lived long enough","He regretted his sufferings"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:24",verse:"But none of these things move me, neither count I my life dear unto myself, so that I might finish my course with joy, and the ministry, which I have received of the Lord Jesus, to testify the gospel of the grace of God.", insight:"A life not counted dear is a life fully spent; joy at the finish line is worth every cost on the road."},
{q:"What did Paul take the elders to record that day?", options:["That he was pure from the blood of all men","That he had never suffered","That he was the greatest apostle","That he would return soon"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:26",verse:"Wherefore I take you to record this day, that I am pure from the blood of all men.", insight:"Pure from their blood because he warned them all; a clear conscience is the preacher's best farewell."},
{q:"What had Paul not shunned to declare?", options:["All the counsel of God","Only the pleasant parts","The traditions of the elders","The wisdom of the Greeks"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:27",verse:"For I have not shunned to declare unto you all the counsel of God.", insight:"All the counsel, not selected slices; the whole counsel is this game's namesake and the preacher's charge."},
{q:"What charge did Paul give the elders?", options:["Take heed to the flock, to feed the church of God which He purchased with His own blood","Build a larger meeting hall","Appoint new deacons at once","Write to the churches of Asia"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:28",verse:"Take heed therefore unto yourselves, and to all the flock, over the which the Holy Ghost hath made you overseers, to feed the church of God, which he hath purchased with his own blood.", insight:"The flock was bought with blood; shepherds handle something infinitely precious."},
{q:"What did Paul warn would happen after his departing?", options:["Grievous wolves would enter in, not sparing the flock","Peace would settle over Ephesus","The temple would be rebuilt","The Romans would leave Asia"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:29",verse:"For I know this, that after my departing shall grievous wolves enter in among you, not sparing the flock.", insight:"Wolves come after the shepherd leaves; vigilance is the price of a kept flock."},
{q:"How long had Paul warned them?", options:["Three years, night and day with tears","One year, on the sabbaths","Forty days","Seven years"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:31",verse:"Therefore watch, and remember, that by the space of three years I ceased not to warn every one night and day with tears.", insight:"Three years of tearful warning; love that warns is love that lasts."},
{q:"What words of the Lord Jesus did Paul quote?", options:["It is more blessed to give than to receive","Blessed are the peacemakers","Ask, and it shall be given you","Come unto me, all ye that labour"], a:0, ref:"Acts 20:35",verse:"I have shewed you all things, how that so labouring ye ought to support the weak, and to remember the words of the Lord Jesus, how he said, It is more blessed to give than to receive.", insight:"The only saying of Jesus found in Acts but not in the Gospels; giving outruns getting in the kingdom."}
]}
]);
